<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<section xml:id="chapter11_catching-exceptions-safely">
    <title>Catching Exceptions Safely</title>

    <p>
        A program may provide more than one handler for a <c>try</c> block.
        Good handlers preserve the type information of the exception, respond
        to specific failures first, and avoid silently hiding a problem.
    </p>

    <subsection xml:id="chapter11_catch-by-const-reference">
        <title>Catch by <c>const</c> Reference</title>

        <p>
            The usual form for catching a standard exception is
            <c>catch (const std::exception&amp; error)</c>. The reference avoids
            copying the exception object, and <c>const</c> promises that the
            handler will not modify it. Catching by reference also preserves the
            actual derived exception type when a general base-class handler is
            used.
        </p>

        <listing xml:id="listing_chapter11_catch-by-reference">
            <title>Catching a standard exception by constant reference</title>
            <program xml:id="program_chapter11_catch-by-reference"
                     language="cpp"
                     interactive="yes"
                     line-numbers="yes">
                <code><![CDATA[
#include <iostream>
#include <stdexcept>

int main() {
    try {
        throw std::runtime_error("the operation failed");
    }
    catch (const std::exception& error) {
        std::cout << error.what() << '\n';
    }

    return 0;
}
                ]]></code>
            </program>
        </listing>

        <p>
            The handler can use the common interface supplied by
            <c>std::exception</c>, including <c>what()</c>, even though the
            thrown object is specifically a <c>std::runtime_error</c>.
        </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="chapter11-specific-before-general">
        <title>Catch Specific Types Before General Types</title>

        <p>
            A derived exception can match a handler for its base class. For that
            reason, handlers should normally be ordered from specific to
            general. A handler for <c>std::invalid_argument</c> should appear
            before a handler for <c>std::exception</c>. If the general handler
            appears first, it may catch the exception before the specific
            handler can respond.
        </p>

        <listing xml:id="listing_chapter11_handler-order">
            <title>Specific and general handlers</title>
            <program xml:id="program_chapter11_handler-order"
                     language="cpp"
                     interactive="yes"
                     line-numbers="yes">
                <code><![CDATA[
#include <iostream>
#include <stdexcept>

void report_failure(int choice) {
    if (choice == 1) {
        throw std::invalid_argument("invalid argument");
    }

    throw std::runtime_error("operation failed");
}

int main() {
    try {
        report_failure(1);
    }
    catch (const std::invalid_argument& error) {
        std::cout << "Argument problem: " << error.what() << '\n';
    }
    catch (const std::exception& error) {
        std::cout << "Other standard exception: " << error.what() << '\n';
    }

    return 0;
}
                ]]></code>
            </program>
        </listing>

        <p>
            The first handler is selected for <c>std::invalid_argument</c>.
            If the call instead throws <c>std::runtime_error</c>, the second
            handler provides a general response.
        </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="chapter11_exception-handling-guidance">
        <title>Do Not Hide Failures</title>

        <p>
            A handler should do something meaningful: report the problem,
            recover with a valid alternative, record diagnostic information, or
            pass the failure to a higher-level handler. An empty handler can
            make a serious problem appear to have succeeded.
        </p>

        <p>
            Catching <c>std::exception</c> is useful as a final fallback, but a
            program should not use it to erase all information about the cause.
            When possible, handle a specific exception where the program knows
            how to recover and use a general handler only where a general
            response is appropriate.
        </p>

        <table xml:id="table_chapter11_exception-catching-guidance">
            <title>Guidance for exception handlers</title>
            <tabular top="major" left="major" bottom="major" right="major">
                <col width="30%"/>
                <col width="70%"/>
                <row header="yes">
                    <cell><p>Practice</p></cell>
                    <cell><p>Reason</p></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell><p>Catch by <c>const</c> reference.</p></cell>
                    <cell><p>Avoids an unnecessary copy and preserves exception type information.</p></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell><p>Catch specific types before general types.</p></cell>
                    <cell><p>Specific handlers need an opportunity to match first.</p></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell><p>Use <c>what()</c> or another meaningful response.</p></cell>
                    <cell><p>Users and developers need to know what failed.</p></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell><p>Do not catch and ignore an exception accidentally.</p></cell>
                    <cell><p>Silent failure makes programs difficult to trust and debug.</p></cell>
                </row>
            </tabular>
        </table>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="chapter11_exception-visualization-catching">
        <title>Tracing Handler Selection</title>

        <p>
            The static listing above demonstrates handler selection. Because the thrown object is an <c>std::invalid_argument</c>, the specific handler is selected before the general <c>std::exception</c> handler.
        </p>
    </subsection>

    <exercise label="chapter11_catching-exercise-reference">
        <statement>
            <p>Why is <c>catch (const std::exception&amp; error)</c> a common form?</p>
        </statement>
        <choices randomize="yes">
            <choice>
                <statement><p>It automatically fixes the failed operation.</p></statement>
                <feedback><p>The handler still needs to decide how to respond.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice correct="yes">
                <statement><p>It avoids an unnecessary copy and preserves the exception's type relationship.</p></statement>
                <feedback><p>The reference avoids copying, while <c>const</c> prevents modification.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><p>It prevents all exceptions from being thrown.</p></statement>
                <feedback><p>The handler runs after an exception has already been thrown.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                            <statement><p>It makes the handler receive a copy of every exception.</p></statement>
                            <feedback><p>Catching by reference avoids an unnecessary copy.</p></feedback>
                        </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><p>It catches only integer exceptions.</p></statement>
                <feedback><p>The parameter type is a reference to <c>std::exception</c>, not an integer.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
        </choices>
    </exercise>

    <exercise label="chapter11_catching-exercise-order">
        <statement>
            <p>Which handler should appear first?</p>
        </statement>
        <choices randomize="yes">
            <choice>
                            <statement><p>catch (const std::runtime_error&amp; error)</p></statement>
                            <feedback><p>A general handler should not replace a more specific handler that can respond appropriately.</p></feedback>
                        </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><p><c>catch (const int&amp; error)</c></p></statement>
                <feedback><p>This handler is unrelated to the standard exception hierarchy.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice correct="yes">
                <statement><p><c>catch (const std::invalid_argument&amp; error)</c></p></statement>
                <feedback><p>The specific derived type should be checked before the general base type.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><p>No handler should appear first.</p></statement>
                <feedback><p>Handlers must have an order, and specific handlers should come first.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><p><c>catch (const std::exception&amp; error)</c></p></statement>
                <feedback><p>A general base-class handler could catch the exception before the specific handler.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
        </choices>
    </exercise>

    <exercise label="chapter11_catching-parsons-order">
        <statement>
            <p>Arrange the handlers from the specific exception to the general exception.</p>
        </statement>
        <blocks>
            <block order="1"><cline>catch (const std::invalid_argument&amp; error) {</cline></block>
            <block order="2"><cline>std::cout &lt;&lt; error.what() &lt;&lt; '\n';</cline></block>
            <block order="3"><cline>}</cline></block>
            <block order="4"><cline>catch (const std::exception&amp; error) {</cline></block>
            <block order="5"><cline>std::cout &lt;&lt; error.what() &lt;&lt; '\n';</cline></block>
            <block order="6"><cline>}</cline></block>
        </blocks>
    </exercise>

    <exercise label="chapter11_catching-exercise-response">
        <statement>
            <p>Which is a meaningful response for an exception handler?</p>
        </statement>
        <choices randomize="yes">
            <choice>
                <statement><p>Ignore every exception without recording anything.</p></statement>
                <feedback><p>Silent handlers make failures difficult to diagnose.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><p>Assume the failed operation succeeded.</p></statement>
                <feedback><p>The handler should not pretend that an unsuccessful operation completed.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                            <statement><p>Continue as though the failed operation produced a valid result.</p></statement>
                            <feedback><p>Continuing with an invalid result can make the program unsafe.</p></feedback>
                        </choice>
            <choice correct="yes">
                <statement><p>Report the problem or recover with a valid alternative.</p></statement>
                <feedback><p>A handler should respond deliberately rather than silently hiding the failure.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><p>Delete the exception type from the standard library.</p></statement>
                <feedback><p>A handler responds to an exception; it does not change the library.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
        </choices>
    </exercise>
</section>
